WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
This closes out the advanced track — a single reference episode that pulls every privacy and security point made across the whole series into one checklist, before you show this to anyone else.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.043
Start with what leaves the machine at all — restated from episode twelve as one table.

3
00:00:12.043 --> 00:00:14.205
Local models and local embeddings never leave.

4
00:00:14.205 --> 00:00:17.308
A cloud model call sends your prompt and context to that provider.

5
00:00:17.308 --> 00:00:19.000
Nothing else phones home on its own.

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.114
Five things worth being careful with: terminal tool reach — how much of the filesystem it can touch.

7
00:00:23.114 --> 00:00:25.994
Browser session delegation — it can act inside your logged-in browser.

8
00:00:25.994 --> 00:00:28.133
Unattended schedules — running with nobody watching.

9
00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:29.408
The mesh's plaintext transport.

10
00:00:29.408 --> 00:00:32.000
And passive memory accumulation, quietly building up over time.

11
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:43.310
Let's build private-by-default, live: a local model instead of cloud, local embeddings, Kokoro or Piper for speech instead of a cloud voice, Whisper for transcription, and the mesh either off entirely or restricted to LAN-only.

12
00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:46.000
Five switches, and nothing needs to leave the machine.

13
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:54.617
After the fact, you can review exactly what an agent did: read the tool-call cards right in the chat, or grep the conversations folder under home dot harnessx for a tool name or a keyword.

14
00:00:54.617 --> 00:00:57.000
Nothing about an agent's actions is hidden from you.

15
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.396
Last point: sharing a conversation export is safe in one specific sense and risky in another.

16
00:01:01.396 --> 00:01:05.745
It contains everything the model read — never your keys, those stay in the credential store.

17
00:01:05.745 --> 00:01:08.960
So the risk isn't credential leakage, it's your codebase's contents.

18
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.000
Read before you share.

19
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.714
That's the advanced track, complete.

20
00:01:11.714 --> 00:01:17.000
Track D starts now, for anyone who wants to open the codebase itself — beginning with a tour of the repository.

